id: rule-seq-two-increments
title: Two increments
topic: Sequential composition
example-of: SEQ
derived: true

task:
  Use the rule SEQ to derive this triple. The intermediate assertion $R1$
  between the two statements is computed backwards from $Q$.

pre: x = 0
post: x = 3

scheme: SEQ
  First premise: {«P»} x := x + 1 {«R1»}
  Second premise: {«R1»} x := x + 2 {«Q»}
  ---
  {«P»} x := x + 1; x := x + 2 {«Q»}

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  x := x + 1;    // i1
  {R1}
  x := x + 2     // i2
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := x + 2
part: Second premise
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i2
text:
  We substitute the variable $x$ with the assignment expression $x + 2$ in
  $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 2, Q)
  = (x = 3)[x <- x + 2]     // Q is x = 3
  = x + 2 = 3               // x replaced by x + 2
  = x = 1                   // subtract 2 from both sides
set: R1 = x = 1

step: R2, the weakest precondition of x := x + 1
part: First premise
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the variable $x$ with the assignment expression $x + 1$ in
  $R1$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, R1)
  = (x = 1)[x <- x + 1]     // R1 is x = 1
  = x + 1 = 1               // x replaced by x + 1
  = x = 0                   // subtract 1 from both sides
set: R2 = x = 0

step: P implies R2
part: First premise
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the first premise holds when $P$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R2»
vc: x = 0 ==> x = 0
why:
  The two formulas are the same. Both premises hold.
